\subsection{Verifying that every listed submission has a user}

Now that we have seen the command end to end, we can verify the property that
ties its two halves together: whatever the user filter says, every submission
that reaches the printing step must carry a [[user]] attribute.
We stub out the three Canvas round-trips---the assignment lookup, the user
lookup and the submission listing---so that the test exercises only the
resolution and filtering logic in between.
The stubbed submission deliberately has no [[user]] attribute, mirroring what
the listing helpers return, so the test fails loudly if the filtering step is
skipped.

Contrasting the empty regex with the default [[.*]] shows that the two spell
the same request:

<<test functions>>=
class TestListCommandUserResolution:
    """Test user resolution in submissions_list_command()"""

    def run_list_command(self, monkeypatch, user_regex):
        """Run the list command with all Canvas access stubbed out"""
        import argparse
        import types
        from unittest.mock import MagicMock

        import canvaslms.cli.submissions as submissions_module

        user = MagicMock()
        user.id = 1
        user.name = "Alice Anderson"

        assignment = MagicMock()
        assignment.course.course_code = "DD1234"
        assignment.name = "Lab 1"

        # As the listing helpers return it: no user attached yet
        submission = types.SimpleNamespace(user_id=1, assignment=assignment,
                                           grade="P", submitted_at=None,
                                           graded_at=None)

        monkeypatch.setattr(submissions_module.assignments,
                            "process_assignment_option",
                            lambda canvas, args: [assignment])
        monkeypatch.setattr(submissions_module.users,
                            "process_user_or_group_option",
                            lambda canvas, args: [user])
        monkeypatch.setattr(submissions_module, "list_submissions",
                            lambda *args, **kwargs: [submission])

        args = argparse.Namespace(user=user_regex, category=None, group=None,
                                  history=False, ungraded=False,
                                  login_id=False, delimiter="\t")
        submissions_module.submissions_list_command(None, None, args)

    def test_empty_user_regex_matches_everyone(self, monkeypatch, capsys):
        """An explicit -u '' must filter, not skip filtering"""
        self.run_list_command(monkeypatch, "")

        assert "Alice Anderson" in capsys.readouterr().out

    def test_default_user_regex_matches_everyone(self, monkeypatch, capsys):
        """The default -u '.*' asks for the same thing"""
        self.run_list_command(monkeypatch, ".*")

        assert "Alice Anderson" in capsys.readouterr().out
@


\section{Computing the SpeedGrader URL}

Sometimes we want to compute the URL for SpeedGrader for a submission.
The [[Submission]] objects come with an attribute [[preview_url]].
We want to turn that one into the SpeedGrader URL.
The [[preview_url]] looks like this:
\begin{minted}{text}
https://canvas.kth.se/courses/26930/assignments/173825/submissions/108849?
  preview=1&version=5
\end{minted}
It should be turned into this:
\begin{minted}{text}
https://canvas.kth.se/courses/26930/gradebook/speed_grader?
  assignment_id=173825&student_id=108849
\end{minted}
We use Python's regex abilities to rewrite the URL.

Not every submission has a preview URL, though, and then there is no SpeedGrader
page to point at either.
We return [[None]] for those, which is the contract the callers must respect;
\cref{MissingPreviewURL} explains which submissions those are, and why detecting
them is trickier than it looks.
<<functions>>=
def speedgrader(submission):
  """
  Returns the SpeedGrader URL of the submission.
  Returns None if the submission has no preview URL.
  """
  <<return [[None]] when there is no preview URL>>

  speedgrader_url = re.sub("assignments/",
    "gradebook/speed_grader?assignment_id=",
    speedgrader_url)

  speedgrader_url = re.sub("/submissions/",
    "&student_id=",
    speedgrader_url)

  speedgrader_url = re.sub(r"\?preview.*$", "", speedgrader_url)

  return speedgrader_url
@

\subsection{Submissions without a preview URL}\label{MissingPreviewURL}

Canvas attaches [[preview_url]] to the current version of a submission only.
The historical versions in [[submission_history]] therefore arrive without one,
and those are exactly the submissions that [[format_submission]] renders for
[[canvaslms submission --history]] and [[--diff]].
So the empty case is not an exotic corner: it is one whole command.

Detecting it used to be a matter of catching the failed attribute access:
\begin{minted}{python}
try:
  speedgrader_url = submission.preview_url
except AttributeError:
  return None
\end{minted}
That guard was correct as long as \enquote{Canvas did not send this field} meant
\enquote{this Python object has no such attribute}.
Normalizing Canvas objects at ingress broke that equivalence.
[[ensure_submission_attrs]] lists [[preview_url]] among [[SUBMISSION_ATTRS]] and
fills in every missing attribute with [[None]], and [[format_submission]]
normalizes each submission before formatting it.
By the time [[speedgrader]] is called, the attribute always exists.

The guard thus became dead code without any change to [[speedgrader]] itself:
[[AttributeError]] can no longer be raised, so [[None]] flows past the [[try]]
block into [[re.sub]], which raises [[TypeError]] instead
(\enquote{expected string or bytes-like object}).
The failure mode moved from \enquote{handled} to \enquote{crash} while the
handling code still looked right.

The general lesson is worth stating, because it applies to every guard written
against a Canvas attribute: once objects are normalized at ingress, absence is a
\emph{value}, not a missing attribute, so guards must test the value.
We test for a falsy URL, which covers both the normalized [[None]] and an empty
string from Canvas.
We still read the attribute with a [[getattr]] default, because [[speedgrader]]
is a small public helper that callers may reach with an object that never passed
through normalization; falling back to [[None]] keeps its documented contract in
that case too.
<<return [[None]] when there is no preview URL>>=
speedgrader_url = getattr(submission, "preview_url", None)
if not speedgrader_url:
  return None
@

\subsection{Verifying [[speedgrader]] URL generation}

The [[speedgrader]] function transforms a preview URL into a SpeedGrader URL.
This is critical for providing direct links to the grading interface.

Let's verify the URL transformation works correctly:

<<test functions>>=
class TestSpeedgraderURL:
