\subsection{One unconvertible attachment must not hide the others}
\label{AttachmentFailureIsolation}

[[convert_to_md]] can fail in many ways: the download can time out, the cached
copy can fail to decrypt, an external converter can be missing, a file can
refuse to decode.
The loop that calls it, however, sits inside a single
[[try]]\ldots[[except AttributeError]] that exists for an entirely different
reason---to skip submissions that carry no [[attachments]] at all.
The scope of that guard, not its intent, is the problem: an exception raised
while converting the third of five files unwinds past the rest of the loop, so
files four and five are never converted.
In JSON output the damage is larger still, because the [[update]] that
introduces the [[attachments]] key runs after the loop: the key never appears,
and the reader has no way to tell an aborted conversion from a submission
without files.

Narrowing the outer guard would not help---it is the loop body that needs to be
recoverable, not the guard that needs to be stricter.
[[extract_submission_text]] (\cref{TextExtractionForDiff}) already demonstrates
the shape we want: it wraps each attachment on its own and substitutes a
placeholder for the ones it cannot convert, so a failure costs exactly one file.
We give this loop the same property.

We catch [[Exception]] rather than any specific class because the loop's
contract is about output, not about diagnosis: whatever goes wrong with one
file, the remaining files must still be rendered.

The placeholder here says more than the one used for diffs, and deliberately so.
That one feeds a comparison between two submission versions, where a message
varying with the error would surface as a spurious change; a fixed string keeps
the diff honest.
This text, by contrast, is read by a human who needs to know why a file is
missing, so we name the error and also warn on standard error.
<<let [[contents]] be the converted [[attachment]]>>=
try:
  contents = convert_to_md(attachment, tmpdir)
except Exception as err:
  canvaslms.cli.warn(
    f"could not convert attachment {attachment.filename}: {err}")
  contents = f"[Could not convert attachment: {err}]"
@

Let's verify that a failing attachment costs us only itself.
The first attachment is unconvertible and the second is fine; both must appear
in the output, and the JSON output must still carry its [[attachments]] key.
<<test functions>>=
class TestAttachmentConversionFailures:
    """Test that one failing attachment does not hide the others."""

    def make_submission(self):
        """Build a submission with a broken and a working attachment."""
        submission = Mock()
        submission.ensure_attrs.return_value = submission
        submission.id = 1
        submission.user_id = 2
        submission.grader_id = None
        submission.grade = "P"
        submission.submitted_at = None
        submission.graded_at = None
        submission.body = None
        submission.submission_data = None
        submission.rubric_assessment = None
        submission.submission_comments = None
        submission.attachments = [Mock(filename="broken.bin"),
                                  Mock(filename="essay.txt")]
        submission.preview_url = (
            "https://example.com/courses/1/assignments/2/submissions/3")
        submission.assignment.name = "A1"
        submission.assignment.course.course_code = "C1"
        return submission

    def convert_all_but_the_first(self, monkeypatch):
        """Make conversion fail for broken.bin and succeed otherwise."""
        def convert_to_md(attachment, tmpdir):
            if attachment.filename == "broken.bin":
                raise AttributeError("content-type")
            return "essay contents"

        monkeypatch.setattr("canvaslms.cli.submissions.convert_to_md",
                            convert_to_md)

    def test_working_attachment_survives_broken_one(self, tmp_path,
                                                    monkeypatch):
        """The good file is converted even though the bad one failed."""
        self.convert_all_but_the_first(monkeypatch)

        output = format_submission(self.make_submission(), tmpdir=tmp_path)

        assert "## broken.bin" in output
        assert "Could not convert attachment" in output
        assert "## essay.txt" in output
        assert "essay contents" in output

    def test_json_output_keeps_attachments_key(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
        """A failed conversion must not drop the whole attachments key."""
        self.convert_all_but_the_first(monkeypatch)

        output = format_submission(self.make_submission(), json_format=True,
                                   tmpdir=tmp_path)

        assert set(output["attachments"]) == {"broken.bin", "essay.txt"}
        assert output["attachments"]["essay.txt"] == "essay contents"
