We check if the attachment is already cached before downloading.
The cache stores attachments encrypted, so there is no file to copy from;
instead [[restore_cached_attachment]] decrypts the content straight into
[[outfile]].
If that reports a miss, we download the file and add it to the cache.

This is particularly important for computing diffs between submission versions:
if a student submits version N+1, we already have the attachments from versions
N, N-1, \ldots cached locally, so we only need to download version N+1.

<<download [[attachment]] to [[outfile]] in [[tmpdir]]>>=
outfile = tmpdir / attachment.filename

if not attachment_cache.restore_cached_attachment(attachment.id, outfile):
  # Download and cache for future use
  attachment.download(outfile)
  attachment_cache.cache_attachment(attachment.id, outfile, {
    'filename': attachment.filename,
    'size': getattr(attachment, 'size', 0),
    'content_type': getattr(attachment, 'content-type',
                            DEFAULT_CONTENT_TYPE)
  })
@

\subsection{Attachments of unknown type}

Canvas does not promise a [[content-type]] on every attachment, which is why
the cache metadata above falls back to [[application/octet-stream]], the
conventional \enquote{unknown binary} type.
The read that drives the conversion has to make the same allowance, and it did
not: an attachment lacking the attribute raised [[AttributeError]] from the
middle of [[convert_to_md]], and that exception did not merely cost us the one
file (\cref{AttachmentFailureIsolation}).

Falling back to [[application/octet-stream]] is not a guess about the file, it
is a statement that we know nothing about it.
That is exactly the right input for the branches below: neither the text branch
nor the PDF branch claims that type, so an attachment of unknown type falls
through to Pandoc, which infers the format from the file's own contents rather
than from Canvas' metadata.
We name the default instead of repeating the literal, because the defect was
precisely that two reads of the same attribute disagreed about it.
<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_CONTENT_TYPE = "application/octet-stream"
<<let [[content_type]] be the attachment's content type>>=
content_type = getattr(attachment, "content-type", DEFAULT_CONTENT_TYPE)
@

Let's verify that an attachment without a content type reaches the converters
instead of raising.

<<test functions>>=
class TestConvertToMdWithoutContentType:
    """Test conversion of attachments that carry no content type."""

    class Attachment:
        """An attachment as Canvas returns it without a content type."""

