If the content type is text, we can just decode it and use it in a Markdown
code block with a suitable (Markdown) content type.
This means that we can set what type of data the code block contains.
We compute this text from the content type of the attachment.
For instance, Python source code is [[text/x-python]].
We remove the [[text/]] prefix and check if there is any [[x-]] prefix left, in 
which case we remove that as well.

Now, we want to change the content type to the format expected by Markdown to 
do proper syntax highlighting.
This requires some ugly processing since one [[.py]] file might have content 
type [[text/x-python]] and another [[.py]] file might have 
[[text/python-script]].
<<functions>>=
def text_to_md(content_type):
  """
  Takes a text-based content type, returns Markdown code block type.
  Raises ValueError if not possible.
  """
  if content_type.startswith("text/"):
    content_type = content_type[len("text/"):]
  else:
    raise ValueError(f"Not text-based content type: {content_type}")

  if content_type.startswith("x-"):
    content_type = content_type[2:]
  if content_type == "python-script":
    content_type = "python"

  return content_type
@

This leaves us with the following.
The advantage of reading the content from the file is that Python will solve 
the encoding for us.
Instead of using an [[if]] statement, we'll go all Python and use a 
[[try-except]] block.
<<if [[content_type]] is text, just use [[outfile]] contents>>=
try:
  md_type = text_to_md(content_type)
  with open(outfile, "r") as f:
    contents = f.read()
  return f"```{md_type}\n{contents}\n```"
except ValueError:
  pass
@

Now we'll do the same for PDF files.
Here we'll use an if statement rather than a [[try]] block.
We'll check for the content type to end with [[pdf]], that will capture also
[[x-pdf]].

We extract the text with pdfminer.six, which we call through its
[[extract_text]] function.  This deserves a word of explanation, because we
used to do the same job by shelling out to [[pdf2txt]] instead---the
command-line front end to that very same library.  Spawning a process was the
worse choice for two reasons that reinforce each other.

The first is that it made pdfminer.six an \emph{undeclared} dependency.
Nothing in our packaging metadata mentioned it, so [[pip]] never installed it,
and whether PDF conversion worked at all came down to what happened to be on
the user's machine.  The maintainer asked for the obvious remedy---declare the
dependency---so pdfminer.six now sits alongside [[pypandoc]] in
[[pyproject.toml]] and is installed with us.

The second reason is why declaring it is not enough on its own, and why we
must import the library rather than keep calling the program.  A dependency
buys us a guarantee about the \emph{package}, not about any executable it
drops on [[PATH]]: upstream installs the front end as [[pdf2txt.py]], Debian
and its derivatives rename it to [[pdf2txt]], and a user with several Python
environments may have neither on [[PATH]] even with the library importable in
ours.  Hard-coding one of those spellings is a guess we cannot win.  Importing
[[extract_text]] makes the question disappear: the name we depend on is a
Python symbol resolved through our own interpreter, which is exactly the thing
the dependency declaration does guarantee.

Extraction can still fail on a corrupt or encrypted PDF, and when it does we
want the same outcome as before: fall through to the [[pypandoc]] attempt
below, and failing that, the pointer to the file on disk.  So we catch
[[Exception]] rather than pdfminer's own [[PDFException]] base class.

The wider net is deliberate, and it is not merely defensive.  We hand
[[extract_text]] a path, so it does the opening itself, which means a file
that has gone missing or is unreadable surfaces as [[FileNotFoundError]] or
[[PermissionError]]---plain [[OSError]] subclasses that lie entirely outside
the [[PDFException]] hierarchy.  Those are reachable states for us, not
hypothetical ones: [[outfile]] lives in a temporary directory that the caller
supplies and that nothing stops an unlucky system from cleaning underneath us.
Catching only [[PDFException]] would let them escape.

That is the same shape of mistake this branch made before.  The old code
caught only [[subprocess.CalledProcessError]], so a missing [[pdf2txt]] raised
[[FileNotFoundError]] straight past it, out of [[convert_to_md]], and past the
attachment loop, which guards only against [[AttributeError]]---aborting the
entire [[submission]] command over one attachment.  In both cases the catch
was narrower than the contract it was meant to implement, which is not
\enquote{the PDF was malformed} but \enquote{this converter cannot produce
text for this attachment}.  Matching the catch to the contract costs us
nothing, because every path out of it still leads somewhere sensible: the next
link in the chain is strictly more capable at guessing formats, and the last
one always succeeds.  The [[pypandoc]] attempt just below already catches
[[Exception]] for exactly this reason.
<<else if [[content_type]] is PDF, convert [[outfile]] using [[extract_text]]>>=
if content_type.endswith("pdf"):
  try:
    return extract_text(outfile)
  except Exception:
    pass
@

Finally, as a last attempt, we use [[pypandoc]] to try to convert it to 
Markdown.
Here we'll use Pandoc's ability to infer the file type on its own.
This means we'll have to download the attachment as a file in a temporary 
location and let Pandoc convert the file to Markdown.
<<else convert [[outfile]] using [[pypandoc]]>>=
try:
  return pypandoc.convert_file(outfile, "markdown")
except Exception as err:
  return f"Cannot convert this file. " \
         f"The file is located at\n\n  {outfile}\n\n"
@

Now that the whole chain is in place, let's verify that a PDF which defeats
[[extract_text]] really does end up in that last-resort message rather than
escaping as an exception.  We pretend the attachment is already cached, so
nothing is downloaded, and then make both converters fail.  The exception we
raise from [[extract_text]] is the [[FileNotFoundError]] it really does raise
when the file has gone missing---deliberately \emph{not} one of pdfminer's own
error types, so the test pins down the part of the catch that a narrower
[[except PDFException]] would get wrong.

<<test functions>>=
class TestPDFConversionFallback:
    """Test that a failing PDF converter degrades to the pointer message"""

    def test_pdf_extraction_failure_falls_through(self, tmp_path,
                                                  monkeypatch):
        """convert_to_md returns the pointer message instead of raising"""
        def cannot_convert(*args, **kwargs):
            raise FileNotFoundError(2, "No such file or directory")

        monkeypatch.setattr(
            "canvaslms.cli.submissions.attachment_cache"
            ".restore_cached_attachment",
            lambda *args, **kwargs: True)
        monkeypatch.setattr(
            "canvaslms.cli.submissions.extract_text",
            cannot_convert)
        monkeypatch.setattr(
            "canvaslms.cli.submissions.pypandoc.convert_file",
            cannot_convert)

        attachment = Mock()
        attachment.id = 1
        attachment.filename = "report.pdf"
        setattr(attachment, "content-type", "application/pdf")

        contents = convert_to_md(attachment, tmp_path)

        assert contents.startswith("Cannot convert this file")
